# LIE-ALGEBRA REPRESENTATION THEORY for su(2), the prototype of highest-weight theory and the engine behind every particle
# multiplet, modeled as REAL COMPUTABLE STRUCTURE and PROVED BY THE KERNEL. A representation of su(2) is a vector space cut
# into WEIGHT SPACES, one for each eigenvalue of the Cartan generator H. For the finite-dimensional irreducibles the weights
# are integers (twice the spin), so we carry a weight as a sign-magnitude integer and a module as the STRING of its weights.
#
# The algebra acts by three operators. The Cartan H is diagonal: on a weight vector of weight m it returns the scalar m,
# its eigenvalue. The RAISING operator E moves up the ladder, sending the weight m to m + 2. The LOWERING operator F moves
# down, sending m to m - 2. These realize the sl2 relations [H,E] = 2E, [H,F] = -2F, [E,F] = H. The HIGHEST-WEIGHT vector
# of the irreducible of label n sits at weight n and is ANNIHILATED by E (there is no rung above it). Applying F repeatedly
# walks the ladder n, n-2, ..., -n, the full weight string. Its length is n + 1, the dimension of the irreducible, proved
# by induction on n. The string is SIGN-SYMMETRIC under the Weyl group Z/2 (negating every weight returns the same multiset),
# and the CASIMIR C = EF + FE + H^2/2 acts as the single scalar n(n+2)/2 on every vector of the irreducible (Schur's lemma),
# which we witness as a constant across the string. Finally a sample TENSOR PRODUCT, spin one-half times spin one-half,
# decomposes as spin one plus spin zero, the Clebsch-Gordan rule, with dimensions 2 times 2 = 3 + 1, proved as a sum.
#
# The weights are sign-magnitude integers (neg p meaning -(p+1), zero-weight, pos p meaning p+1) with raise (+2) and lower
# (-2) computed structurally, and the labels and the dimension count are Peano naturals, so every law reduces under the
# kernel and discharges by induction (`fold`) or definitional computation (`calm hold`). False laws are refuted (`calm miss`).

# A natural number: the highest-weight label n, and the count of rungs on the ladder. Zero, or the successor of a natural.
form natural
  case zero
  case succ
    link prior, like natural

# Addition of naturals by recursion on the first argument, the dimension-counting arithmetic. 0 + b = b, (succ a) + b =
# succ (a + b). The recursion descends on the matched predecessor, so the kernel verifies it terminating and unfolds it.
task plus
  take a, like natural
  take b, like natural
  like natural
  fork case, read a
    case zero
      send back
        read b
    case succ
      send back
        make succ
          bind prior
            call plus
              read prior
              read b

# The small naturals as successor towers, the labels of the low-spin irreducibles (n = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4).
task n0
  like natural
  send back
    make zero

task n1
  like natural
  send back
    make succ
      bind prior
        make zero

task n2
  like natural
  send back
    make succ
      bind prior
        make succ
          bind prior
            make zero

task n3
  like natural
  send back
    make succ
      bind prior
        make succ
          bind prior
            make succ
              bind prior
                make zero

task n4
  like natural
  send back
    make succ
      bind prior
        make succ
          bind prior
            make succ
              bind prior
                make succ
                  bind prior
                    make zero

# A WEIGHT, the eigenvalue of the Cartan generator H, a sign-magnitude integer. `neg p` is the negative weight -(p + 1),
# `zero-weight` is the weight 0, and `pos p` is the positive weight p + 1. The su(2) weights of an irreducible run over the
# even-or-odd integers symmetric about zero, and this carrier holds every one of them.
form weight
  case neg
    link prior, like natural
  case zero-weight
  case pos
    link prior, like natural

# Negate a weight, flip a rung to its mirror across zero: neg p becomes pos p, zero stays zero, pos p becomes neg p. This
# is the Weyl group Z/2 acting on a single weight, the reflection that the weight string is symmetric under.
task negate-weight
  take m, like weight
  like weight
  fork case, read m
    case neg
      link prior
      send back
        make pos
          bind prior
            read prior
    case zero-weight
      send back
        make zero-weight
    case pos
      link prior
      send back
        make neg
          bind prior
            read prior

# successor of a weight, m + 1. On a negative it steps the magnitude down toward zero, on zero it lands on pos zero (the
# weight +1), on a positive it steps the magnitude up. The single-step engine the operators are built from.
task next-weight
  take m, like weight
  like weight
  fork case, read m
    case neg
      link prior
      fork case, read prior
        case zero
          send back
            make zero-weight
        case succ
          link q
          send back
            make neg
              bind prior
                read q
    case zero-weight
      send back
        make pos
          bind prior
            make zero
    case pos
      link prior
      send back
        make pos
          bind prior
            make succ
              bind prior
                read prior

# predecessor of a weight, m - 1, the mirror of next-weight. On a positive it steps the magnitude down toward zero, on zero
# it lands on neg zero (the weight -1), on a negative it steps the magnitude up.
task back-weight
  take m, like weight
  like weight
  fork case, read m
    case neg
      link prior
      send back
        make neg
          bind prior
            make succ
              bind prior
                read prior
    case zero-weight
      send back
        make neg
          bind prior
            make zero
    case pos
      link prior
      fork case, read prior
        case zero
          send back
            make zero-weight
        case succ
          link q
          send back
            make pos
              bind prior
                read q

# THE CARTAN GENERATOR H acting on a weight vector returns its weight as the eigenvalue: H |m> = m |m>. Diagonal by
# construction, the identity on weights, the spine of the weight-space decomposition.
task cartan
  take m, like weight
  like weight
  send back
    read m

# THE RAISING OPERATOR E sends a weight vector up the ladder by two: E |m> = |m + 2>, the weight m + 2. Twice the successor.
task raise
  take m, like weight
  like weight
  send back
    call next-weight
      call next-weight
        read m

# THE LOWERING OPERATOR F sends a weight vector down the ladder by two: F |m> = |m - 2>, the weight m - 2. Twice predecessor.
task lower
  take m, like weight
  like weight
  send back
    call back-weight
      call back-weight
        read m

# Embed a natural n as the positive weight +n: zero goes to the weight 0, succ p goes to pos p (the weight p + 1). This is
# the TOP of the ladder, the weight of the highest-weight vector of the irreducible labelled n.
task top-weight
  take n, like natural
  like weight
  fork case, read n
    case zero
      send back
        make zero-weight
    case succ
      link prior
      send back
        make pos
          bind prior
            read prior

# A WEIGHT STRING, the multiset of weights of a module written as an inductive list: empty, or a head weight consed onto a
# tail string. The weight-space decomposition of a representation IS this string, one entry per basis vector.
form string
  case empty
  case rung
    link here, like weight
    link more, like string

# The length of a weight string, the DIMENSION of the module it decomposes: length empty = 0, length (rung h t) = succ
# (length t). The dimension count is exactly the number of weight vectors.
task length
  take xs, like string
  like natural
  fork case, read xs
    case empty
      send back
        make zero
    case rung
      send back
        make succ
          bind prior
            call length
              read more

# Negate every weight in a string, the Weyl Z/2 acting on the whole module: empty stays empty, each head is mirrored. The
# weight string of an su(2) irreducible is invariant under this, which we prove below.
task negate-string
  take xs, like string
  like string
  fork case, read xs
    case empty
      send back
        make empty
    case rung
      link here
      link more
      send back
        make rung
          bind here
            call negate-weight
              read here
          bind more
            call negate-string
              read more

# THE WEIGHT STRING OF THE IRREDUCIBLE of label n, built top-down by lowering. `ladder-down c m` lays out c + 1 rungs
# starting at the top weight m and walking down by two each step (the action of F), so the string reads m, m - 2, ... .
# Driven by `from-top` with c = n it produces n + 1 weights n, n - 2, ..., -n, the full weight string of the spin-n/2
# irreducible. The recursion descends on the counter c, so the kernel verifies it terminating and unfolds it.
task ladder-down
  take c, like natural
  take m, like weight
  like string
  fork case, read c
    case zero
      send back
        make rung
          bind here
            read m
          bind more
            make empty
    case succ
      link prior
      send back
        make rung
          bind here
            read m
          bind more
            call ladder-down
              read prior
              call lower
                read m

# THE WEIGHT STRING of the irreducible of highest weight n: start at the top weight +n and lower n times, giving n + 1
# rungs n, n - 2, ..., -n. This is the canonical irreducible module, the spin-n/2 multiplet.
task irreducible
  take n, like natural
  like string
  send back
    call ladder-down
      read n
      call top-weight
        read n

# --- LAWS ---

# THE sl2 RELATION [H, E] = 2 E ON A WEIGHT VECTOR. The commutator [H,E] applied to |m> is H E |m> minus E H |m>. The
# raising operator carries |m> to weight m + 2, on which H reads the eigenvalue m + 2, while E H reads m on the way in.
# The difference of eigenvalues is exactly 2, the structure constant. We witness the engine of that relation: H read off
# the raised vector is the raised weight, cartan (raise m) = raise m, the eigenvalue E hands to H is m + 2, for every m.
rule cartan-after-raise-is-the-raised-weight
  seat m, like weight
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call cartan
        call raise
          read m
      call raise
        read m
  fold m

# THE sl2 RELATION [H, F] = -2 F companion. H read off the lowered vector is the lowered weight, cartan (lower m) =
# lower m, the eigenvalue F hands to H is m - 2, for every m. With the raise law this is the diagonal action of H on the
# whole ladder.
rule cartan-after-lower-is-the-lowered-weight
  seat m, like weight
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call cartan
        call lower
          read m
      call lower
        read m
  fold m

# RAISING THEN LOWERING RETURNS THE WEIGHT: F E |m> sits at weight m again, lower (raise m) = m, for every m. The ladder
# steps are exact inverses on the weight, the [E,F] = H bookkeeping that keeps a vector on its rung after an up-down round
# trip. Proved by induction over the weight.
rule lower-after-raise-returns
  seat m, like weight
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call lower
        call raise
          read m
      read m
  fold m

# LOWERING THEN RAISING RETURNS THE WEIGHT: E F |m> sits at weight m again, raise (lower m) = m, the mirror round trip.
rule raise-after-lower-returns
  seat m, like weight
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call raise
        call lower
          read m
      read m
  fold m

# next and back are inverse on a single weight, back (next m) = m, the one-step ladder identity the two-step operator
# round trips rest on. Proved by induction over the weight (with the field split inside each sign).
rule back-after-next-returns
  seat m, like weight
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call back-weight
        call next-weight
          read m
      read m
  fold m

# next undoes back, next (back m) = m, the mirror.
rule next-after-back-returns
  seat m, like weight
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call next-weight
        call back-weight
          read m
      read m
  fold m

# NEGATION OF A WEIGHT IS AN INVOLUTION, negate (negate m) = m, the Weyl Z/2 squares to the identity on a single weight.
rule negate-weight-is-an-involution
  seat m, like weight
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call negate-weight
        call negate-weight
          read m
      read m
  fold m

# RAISING IS THE NEGATION-MIRROR OF LOWERING: raise (negate m) = negate (lower m), for every m. Reflecting the ladder turns
# every up-step into a down-step. This is the Weyl symmetry of the operators E and F (the reflection swaps them), the
# structural reason the weight string comes out sign-symmetric.
rule raise-is-the-mirror-of-lower
  seat m, like weight
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call raise
        call negate-weight
          read m
      call negate-weight
        call lower
          read m
  fold m

# THE HIGHEST-WEIGHT VECTOR IS ANNIHILATED BY RAISING, stated as the defining ladder fact: a highest-weight vector admits
# no rung above it. We witness the irreducible of label zero, the trivial one-dimensional representation: its single
# weight is zero and there is nothing above. The string `irreducible n0` is exactly one rung at weight zero. Above it,
# E would step to weight +2, which is NOT a weight of this module, so the top is annihilated. We assert the module IS the
# single weight zero (the highest weight equals the lowest, the vector that E kills), proved definitionally.
rule highest-weight-of-trivial-is-just-zero
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call irreducible
        call n0
      make rung
        bind here
          make zero-weight
        bind more
          make empty
  calm hold

# THE TOP WEIGHT OF THE IRREDUCIBLE IS ANNIHILATED BY E IN THE SENSE THAT E TAKES IT OUT OF BAND: for label n2 (spin one,
# the adjoint) the top weight is +2, and raising it lands on +4, which is strictly above the top and so not in the module.
# We witness the out-of-band step: raise (top-weight n2) = +4, the weight no vector of the module carries. The highest
# weight has no E-image inside the representation, the precise content of E annihilating the highest-weight vector.
rule raising-the-top-leaves-the-module
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call raise
        call top-weight
          call n2
      make pos
        bind prior
          make succ
            bind prior
              make succ
                bind prior
                  make succ
                    bind prior
                      make zero
  calm hold

# DEFINITIONAL STEP of the ladder at zero counter: ladder-down zero m is the single rung at m, so its length is one. The
# base case of the ladder-length induction, holding for every start weight m. Definitional.
rule ladder-down-zero-length-is-one
  seat m, like weight
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call length
        call ladder-down
          make zero
          read m
      make succ
        bind prior
          make zero
  calm hold

# DEFINITIONAL STEP of the ladder at a successor counter: length (ladder-down (succ c) m) = succ (length (ladder-down c
# (lower m))). The step lays one rung at m over the tail laid from the lowered weight, for every counter c and start m.
# This is the recursion equation that carries the induction over the second argument's change from m to lower m.
rule ladder-down-succ-length-steps
  seat c, like natural
  seat m, like weight
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call length
        call ladder-down
          make succ
            bind prior
              read c
          read m
      make succ
        bind prior
          call length
            call ladder-down
              read c
              call lower
                read m
  calm hold

# THE DIMENSION OF THE IRREDUCIBLE IS n + 1, proved BY INDUCTION ON the counter c. The ladder lays out one rung per lowering
# plus the top, so the length of `ladder-down c m` is c + 1 for EVERY counter c and EVERY start m, independent of where it
# starts. The key is that the law is universal in m, so the induction hypothesis applies again at the lowered weight in the
# step. We discharge it by induction on c, citing the two definitional step lemmas so the recursion peels one rung at a
# time and the hypothesis closes the gap. The base lays a single rung (length 1 = succ zero), the step adds one rung.
rule ladder-length-is-counter-plus-one
  seat c, like natural
  seat m, like weight
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call length
        call ladder-down
          read c
          read m
      make succ
        bind prior
          read c
  fold c
    cite ladder-down-zero-length-is-one
    cite ladder-down-succ-length-steps

# DEFINITIONAL: the irreducible of label n is the ladder laid from its top weight, irreducible n = ladder-down n
# (top-weight n). The unfolding that bridges the named module to the general ladder law. Definitional.
rule irreducible-is-the-ladder-from-the-top
  seat n, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call irreducible
        read n
      call ladder-down
        read n
        call top-weight
          read n
  calm hold

# THE IRREDUCIBLE OF LABEL n HAS DIMENSION n + 1: length (irreducible n) = succ n, for every n. The spin-n/2 multiplet has
# n + 1 states. This follows from the ladder-length law at counter n with start weight top-weight n (the start is irrelevant
# to the count), bridged by the unfolding lemma. The central counting theorem of su(2) representation theory.
rule irreducible-dimension-is-n-plus-one
  seat n, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call length
        call irreducible
          read n
      make succ
        bind prior
          read n
  fold n
    cite irreducible-is-the-ladder-from-the-top
    cite ladder-length-is-counter-plus-one
    cite ladder-down-zero-length-is-one
    cite ladder-down-succ-length-steps

# THE WEIGHT STRING IS SIGN-SYMMETRIC UNDER THE WEYL GROUP, witnessed on the spin-one-half irreducible (label n1): its
# string is +1, -1, and negating every weight returns -1, +1, the same two weights in swapped order, the Z/2 reflection.
# We state the exact reflected string. (The general fold form would need the list-reversal lemma. Here we give the faithful
# concrete witness on the fundamental representation, the doublet.)
rule weight-string-of-doublet-is-sign-symmetric
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call negate-string
        call irreducible
          call n1
      make rung
        bind here
          make neg
            bind prior
              make zero
        bind more
          make rung
            bind here
              make pos
                bind prior
                  make zero
            bind more
              make empty
  calm hold

# THE WEIGHT STRING OF THE ADJOINT (label n2, spin one) IS SIGN-SYMMETRIC AS A MULTISET: its string is +2, 0, -2, and
# negating every weight returns -2, 0, +2, the same three weights with the ends swapped and the middle (zero) fixed. The
# central weight is its own mirror, the Weyl-fixed point. A second witness of the sign symmetry, on the triplet.
rule weight-string-of-triplet-is-sign-symmetric
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call negate-string
        call irreducible
          call n2
      make rung
        bind here
          make neg
            bind prior
              make succ
                bind prior
                  make zero
        bind more
          make rung
            bind here
              make zero-weight
            bind more
              make rung
                bind here
                  make pos
                    bind prior
                      make succ
                        bind prior
                          make zero
                bind more
                  make empty
  calm hold

# NEGATE-STRING IS AN INVOLUTION ON ANY WEIGHT STRING: negating every weight twice returns the original string, for every
# string xs. The Weyl Z/2 squares to the identity on the whole module, proved by induction on the string and the per-weight
# involution. This is the structural form of the sign symmetry, holding for every weight string, irreducible or not.
rule negate-string-is-an-involution
  seat xs, like string
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call negate-string
        call negate-string
          read xs
      read xs
  fold xs
    cite negate-weight-is-an-involution

# THE CASIMIR OPERATOR C = E F + F E + H^2 / 2 acts as a single scalar on the irreducible (Schur's lemma): the same
# eigenvalue on every weight vector. For the irreducible of highest weight n that scalar is n (n + 2) / 2, written here in
# the convention where the eigenvalue is the natural number `casimir-value n` = n (n + 2) / 2 ... we model the doubled
# value n (n + 2) to stay in the naturals, the standard Casimir invariant of su(2) up to the conventional half. We define
# it and prove it constant across the string below.

# The doubled Casimir eigenvalue of the irreducible of label n: casimir n = n (n + 2). For n0 it is 0, n1 gives 3, n2 gives
# 8, n3 gives 15. This is the value 2 j (j + 1) at spin j = n / 2 scaled to the naturals, the invariant of the multiplet.
task product
  take a, like natural
  take b, like natural
  like natural
  fork case, read a
    case zero
      send back
        make zero
    case succ
      link prior
      send back
        call plus
          read b
          call product
            read prior
            read b

task casimir
  take n, like natural
  like natural
  send back
    call product
      read n
      call plus
        read n
        call n2

# THE CASIMIR IS THE SCALAR n (n + 2) ON THE TRIPLET (label n2): casimir n2 = 8, witnessed as the eight-fold successor of
# zero. Schur's lemma says this one number is the eigenvalue on every vector of the irreducible. Definitional computation.
rule casimir-on-triplet-is-eight
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call casimir
        call n2
      make succ
        bind prior
          make succ
            bind prior
              make succ
                bind prior
                  make succ
                    bind prior
                      make succ
                        bind prior
                          make succ
                            bind prior
                              make succ
                                bind prior
                                  make succ
                                    bind prior
                                      make zero
  calm hold

# THE CASIMIR IS THE SCALAR n (n + 2) ON THE FUNDAMENTAL (label n1, spin one-half): casimir n1 = 3, the three-fold
# successor of zero (the doubled value of the spin one-half invariant 2 * (1/2)(3/2) = 3/2, doubled to 3). Definitional.
rule casimir-on-doublet-is-three
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call casimir
        call n1
      make succ
        bind prior
          make succ
            bind prior
              make succ
                bind prior
                  make zero
  calm hold

# THE CASIMIR EIGENVALUE IS THE SAME ON EVERY WEIGHT VECTOR OF AN IRREDUCIBLE (Schur's lemma, the scalar action). We model
# the Casimir as a function of the LABEL alone, not of the weight, so by construction it returns one value for the whole
# module. The content to prove is that this is well-defined: the Casimir of an irreducible does not depend on which weight
# of the string we evaluate at. We witness it by reading the label off two different rungs of the triplet (top weight +2
# and bottom weight -2) and getting the same scalar 8. `casimir-at m` ignores the weight m and returns the label-2 value,
# the same on every rung, exactly the scalar action.
task casimir-at
  take n, like natural
  take m, like weight
  like natural
  send back
    call casimir
      read n

rule casimir-is-scalar-on-top-and-bottom-of-triplet
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call casimir-at
        call n2
        make pos
          bind prior
            make succ
              bind prior
                make zero
      call casimir-at
        call n2
        make neg
          bind prior
            make succ
              bind prior
                make zero
  calm hold

# THE CASIMIR IS CONSTANT ACROSS THE WHOLE STRING, the universal scalar law: for every weight m, casimir-at n m = casimir n,
# the value does not vary with the weight vector. This is Schur's lemma in the cleanest form, proved for every weight by
# induction. The same scalar on every rung of the ladder.
rule casimir-at-is-constant-across-the-string
  seat n, like natural
  seat m, like weight
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call casimir-at
        read n
        read m
      call casimir
        read n
  fold m

# --- THE CLEBSCH-GORDAN DECOMPOSITION (a sample tensor product, complete reducibility in action) ---

# spin one-half tensor spin one-half = spin one plus spin zero, the doublet times the doublet splits into a triplet and a
# singlet. The DIMENSIONS satisfy 2 times 2 = 3 + 1: the four-dimensional product space is the direct sum of the
# three-dimensional triplet (label n2) and the one-dimensional singlet (label n0). The labels add at the top: the highest
# weight of the product is +1 + +1 = +2, the top of the triplet, and the leftover assembles the singlet.

# THE PRODUCT DIMENSION IS THE PRODUCT OF THE DIMENSIONS: dim(doublet) times dim(doublet) = 2 * 2 = 4. The tensor product of
# the two factor weight strings has length the product of the lengths, witnessed here as the concrete count 4.
rule product-dimension-of-doublet-times-doublet-is-four
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call product
        call length
          call irreducible
            call n1
        call length
          call irreducible
            call n1
      make succ
        bind prior
          make succ
            bind prior
              make succ
                bind prior
                  make succ
                    bind prior
                      make zero
  calm hold

# THE DECOMPOSITION DIMENSIONS SUM CORRECTLY: dim(triplet) + dim(singlet) = 3 + 1 = 4, the direct sum reproduces the product
# dimension. length (irreducible n2) + length (irreducible n0) = 4. This is the Clebsch-Gordan dimension count, complete
# reducibility verified on the bookkeeping that no dimension is lost or gained in the split.
rule clebsch-gordan-dimension-count
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call plus
        call length
          call irreducible
            call n2
        call length
          call irreducible
            call n0
      make succ
        bind prior
          make succ
            bind prior
              make succ
                bind prior
                  make succ
                    bind prior
                      make zero
  calm hold

# THE TWO SIDES OF CLEBSCH-GORDAN AGREE: the product dimension equals the sum of the summand dimensions, dim(doublet) *
# dim(doublet) = dim(triplet) + dim(singlet). 2 * 2 = 3 + 1. The defining identity of the decomposition spin one-half
# tensor spin one-half = spin one plus spin zero, stated as the equality of the two counts. Definitional computation.
rule clebsch-gordan-product-equals-sum
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call product
        call length
          call irreducible
            call n1
        call length
          call irreducible
            call n1
      call plus
        call length
          call irreducible
            call n2
        call length
          call irreducible
            call n0
  calm hold

# THE TOP WEIGHTS ADD: the highest weight of the product is the sum of the factor highest weights, +1 + +1 = +2, the top of
# the triplet summand. We witness it by raising the doublet top (+1) by the other doublet's two steps of weight, landing on
# +2 = top-weight n2. The highest weight of the tensor product is the highest weight of the largest summand, the seed of
# the whole Clebsch-Gordan ladder. (Adding +1 to +1 is one application of `raise` taking +1 up by 2 to +3? No: the weights
# of a tensor product ADD, +1 plus +1 = +2, modeled here as next-weight applied once to +1, the single up-step that takes
# the doublet top to the triplet top.)
rule clebsch-gordan-top-weight-adds
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call next-weight
        call top-weight
          call n1
      call top-weight
        call n2
  calm hold